export const B1={title:'The biotechnology industry: health applications, careers and regulation',hook:'Insulin for people with diabetes once came from the pancreases of pigs and cows. Today most insulin is made by microorganisms carrying a human gene. That shift—from extracting a molecule from animals to engineering cells to produce it—captures what biotechnology is: using living systems and their molecules to make useful products.',objectives:['Define biotechnology and describe its major sectors and health applications.','Describe the stages of the product pipeline from research to manufacturing.','Identify the U.S. agencies that regulate biotechnology products and their roles.','Describe biotechnology careers, education pathways and workplace practices such as GLP and GMP.'],sections:[
['What biotechnology is',[
'**Biotechnology** is the use of living organisms, cells or biological molecules to make products or solve problems. Ancient examples include fermentation to make bread, cheese and yogurt and selective breeding of crops and animals. Modern biotechnology began with **recombinant DNA** in the 1970s, when scientists learned to cut DNA with restriction enzymes and join it into plasmids that bacteria copy. In 1982, human insulin made by genetically engineered bacteria became the first recombinant drug approved in the United States.',
'Biotechnology is often grouped by sector, sometimes using colors: **medical (red)** biotechnology develops drugs, vaccines, diagnostics and gene therapies; **agricultural (green)** biotechnology develops crops with traits such as pest resistance; **industrial (white)** biotechnology uses enzymes and microbes to make chemicals, fuels and materials; and **environmental** biotechnology uses organisms to clean pollution (**bioremediation**). The Bio-Rad reference also introduces **systems biology** disciplines: **genomics** studies the full complement of DNA in a cell, **transcriptomics** all RNA transcripts, **proteomics** all proteins and **metabolomics** all small molecules.',
match('Match the “-omics” field',[['Full complement of DNA in a cell','Genomics'],['All RNA transcripts in a cell','Transcriptomics'],['All proteins in a cell','Proteomics'],['All small-molecule metabolites','Metabolomics'],['Using microbes to clean up pollution','Bioremediation']])]],
['Biotechnology in health',[
'Health applications include **recombinant protein drugs** (insulin, growth hormone, clotting factors), **monoclonal antibodies** for cancer and autoimmune disease, **vaccines** including recombinant and mRNA vaccines, **diagnostics** such as PCR tests and ELISA-based tests, **gene therapy** that delivers a functional gene, **cell therapies** such as CAR-T cells engineered to attack cancer, and **pharmacogenomics**, which uses genetic differences to guide drug choice. Many medicines made by living cells are called **biologics**; they are large, complex molecules, so copies made by other companies are called **biosimilars** rather than generics.',
'These advances raise ethical and practical questions about cost, access, privacy of genetic data, safety of new technologies and fairness in who benefits. Biotechnology professionals work within regulatory systems designed to address safety and effectiveness, while public discussion addresses broader values.']],
['From discovery to market',[
'A typical biotechnology medical product moves through a **pipeline**: **research and discovery** (identifying a target and candidate), **preclinical development** (laboratory and animal studies of safety and activity), an **Investigational New Drug (IND)** application to the FDA, **clinical trials** in people, an application for approval (a **Biologics License Application** for biologics or a New Drug Application for drugs), **manufacturing scale-up** and **post-market surveillance**. Clinical trials proceed in phases: **Phase I** tests safety and dosing in a small group; **Phase II** tests effectiveness and side effects in a larger group; **Phase III** compares the product with standard treatment in large groups; **Phase IV** monitors safety after approval. The process often takes a decade or more, and most candidates never reach approval.',
order('Order the product pipeline',['Research and discovery of a target','Preclinical laboratory and animal studies','Investigational New Drug application to the FDA','Phase I, II and III clinical trials','Biologics License Application and FDA review','Manufacturing scale-up and Phase IV post-market surveillance'])]],
['Who regulates biotechnology',[
'In the United States, the **Coordinated Framework for the Regulation of Biotechnology** (first issued in 1986) divides oversight among existing agencies according to the product rather than the method used to make it. The **Food and Drug Administration (FDA)** regulates human and animal drugs, biologics, medical devices, food safety and food additives. The **U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA)**, through its Animal and Plant Health Inspection Service, regulates plant pests and genetically engineered plants that could pose plant-health risks. The **Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)** regulates pesticides, including plants engineered to produce pesticidal substances, and certain microorganisms. The **National Institutes of Health (NIH)** issues guidelines for research involving recombinant or synthetic nucleic acid molecules, overseen locally by **Institutional Biosafety Committees**, and the **Centers for Disease Control and Prevention** provide biosafety guidance and regulate select agents.',
sort('Which agency has primary oversight?',['FDA','USDA','EPA'],[['A new monoclonal antibody drug',0],['A recombinant vaccine',0],['A genetically engineered crop that could affect plant health',1],['A corn plant engineered to produce its own insecticide protein',2],['A diagnostic test kit',0],['A microbial pesticide',2]])]],
['Careers and quality systems',[
'Biotechnology careers span research, manufacturing, quality and regulation. **Laboratory technicians** and **research associates** carry out experiments; **biomanufacturing technicians** operate bioreactors and purification systems; **quality control analysts** test products; **quality assurance specialists** review processes and records; **regulatory affairs specialists** prepare submissions to agencies; **clinical research associates** monitor trials; **bioinformaticians** analyze genetic and protein data. Entry roles may require a certificate or associate degree; many research and leadership roles require bachelor’s or advanced degrees. The Bureau of Labor Statistics tracks job outlook for biological technicians and related occupations.',
'Regulated laboratories follow formal quality systems. **Good Laboratory Practice (GLP)** governs nonclinical safety studies; **Good Manufacturing Practice (GMP)**, often called current GMP, governs how products are made and tested. Both depend on **standard operating procedures (SOPs)**, calibrated equipment, trained staff and complete, accurate records: “if it isn’t documented, it didn’t happen.” A **laboratory notebook** records date, purpose, materials, procedures, observations and results in permanent ink, with errors crossed out by a single line rather than erased.',
flip('Industry vocabulary',[['Biologic','A medicine made by or from living cells'],['Biosimilar','A highly similar version of an approved biologic'],['IND','Investigational New Drug application to begin human trials'],['BLA','Biologics License Application for approval'],['GMP','Good Manufacturing Practice rules for making products'],['SOP','A written standard operating procedure']])]],
["Ethics, access and public trust",["Biotechnology’s power raises questions that science alone cannot answer. **Access and cost**: many biologics and gene therapies cost tens or hundreds of thousands of dollars, raising concerns about who can benefit. **Genetic privacy**: genomic data can reveal information about relatives and future health risks, so consent and data protection matter. **Safety and the environment**: genetically engineered organisms released into the environment are evaluated for effects on ecosystems, and laboratory work follows biosafety and biosecurity rules to prevent accidental or deliberate misuse. **Human genome editing**: international scientific bodies have urged strict limits on heritable editing of embryos.","Public trust depends on transparency, honest communication of uncertainty and fair inclusion of diverse populations in research. Biotechnology professionals contribute by following regulations, documenting work accurately, reporting adverse events and explaining science clearly. When you study a new product, ask four questions: Is it safe? Does it work? Who regulates it? Who can access it?"]]
],takeaways:['Biotechnology uses living systems and molecules; recombinant DNA began the modern industry, and recombinant insulin was approved in 1982.','Products move from discovery through preclinical work, IND, Phase I–III trials, BLA review and post-market monitoring.','Under the Coordinated Framework, FDA, USDA and EPA regulate by product; GLP, GMP, SOPs and notebooks ensure quality.'],terms:[['Recombinant DNA','DNA combined from different sources, often in a plasmid.'],['Genomics','Study of the full complement of DNA in a cell or organism.'],['Coordinated Framework','The 1986 U.S. policy dividing biotechnology oversight among FDA, USDA and EPA.']],mcqs:[q('Which discipline of systems biology investigates the full complement of DNA in a cell?','Genomics','Proteomics','Microbiomics','Metabolomics','Genomics studies the genome.'),q('The first recombinant drug approved in the United States, in 1982, was:','Human insulin','Penicillin','Aspirin','Polio vaccine','It was produced by engineered bacteria.'),q('Which phase of clinical trials primarily tests safety and dosing in a small group?','Phase I','Phase II','Phase III','Phase IV','Phase I focuses on safety.'),q('Which agency regulates a new monoclonal antibody drug?','FDA','USDA','EPA','OSHA','The FDA regulates biologics.'),q('A plant engineered to produce its own insecticide protein is regulated in part by:','EPA','FDA only','CDC only','NIH only','The EPA regulates pesticidal substances.'),q('A highly similar copy of an approved biologic is called a:','Biosimilar','Generic','Placebo','Prodrug','Biologics are too complex for identical generics.'),q('Good Manufacturing Practice (GMP) governs:','How products are made and tested','Only animal research','Classroom grading','Hospital billing','GMP ensures product quality.'),q('Correct notebook practice for an error is to:','Draw a single line through it so it stays readable','Erase it completely','Tear out the page','Use correction fluid','Records must remain traceable.')],frq:{prompt:'A company has engineered bacteria to make a new therapeutic protein. Describe the stages it must pass through before reaching patients, the agencies and rules that apply, and two career roles that would contribute along the way.',rubric:[['Pipeline stages',['preclinical','ind','phase','clinical','bla','approval']],['Regulation',['fda','coordinated framework','gmp','glp']],['Two careers with roles',['technician','quality','regulatory','research associate','manufacturing','bioinformatic']],['Post-market and documentation',['post-market','phase iv','sop','record','document']]],model:'The protein would go through preclinical laboratory and animal studies under Good Laboratory Practice, then the company would submit an Investigational New Drug application to the FDA before Phase I safety trials, Phase II effectiveness trials and Phase III comparisons with standard treatment. It would then submit a Biologics License Application; the FDA regulates biologics under the Coordinated Framework. Manufacturing must follow Good Manufacturing Practice with SOPs, calibrated equipment and complete records, and Phase IV post-market surveillance continues after approval. Biomanufacturing technicians would grow the bacteria and purify the protein, quality control analysts would test each batch, and regulatory affairs specialists would prepare the FDA submissions.'},sources:[RULES,FDA_BIO,USDA_COORD,BLS_BIO]};

export const B2={title:'Laboratory safety, personal protective equipment and preparing solutions',hook:'Every Biotechnology skill on the HOSA rating sheets begins the same way: enter wearing closed-toed shoes, then put on safety glasses or goggles and gloves. Every skill ends the same way too: dispose of waste, disinfect the bench, remove PPE and wash hands—in that order. Safety and accurate solutions are the foundation of every result in the lab.',objectives:['Apply laboratory safety rules, PPE use and biosafety levels.','Use safety data sheets, labels and waste disposal correctly.','Use a balance correctly, including taring.','Calculate solutions using percent, molarity and dilution formulas.'],sections:[
['Safety culture and PPE',[
'Laboratory safety begins before work starts: know the location of eyewash stations, safety showers, fire extinguishers and exits; never eat, drink or apply cosmetics in the lab; tie back long hair; and wear **closed-toed shoes**. **Personal protective equipment (PPE)** for biotechnology work typically includes **safety glasses or goggles** and **gloves**, with a **lab coat** as appropriate. Gloves protect against chemicals and biological materials but should be removed before touching doorknobs, phones or your face. HOSA’s rating sheets award points for entering properly dressed and donning glasses and gloves, and for the clean-up sequence at the end of every skill.',
order('End-of-skill clean-up sequence (as rated by HOSA)',['Dispose of used pipet tips, tubes and transfer pipets in the waste receptacle','Clean the work area with surface disinfectant','Remove PPE','Wash hands or use alcohol-based hand rub'])]],
['Biosafety levels and hazards',[
'The CDC and NIH describe four **biosafety levels (BSL)**. **BSL-1** is for well-characterized agents not known to cause disease in healthy adults, such as the nonpathogenic *E. coli* K-12 strains used in classroom transformation; standard practices and a sink for handwashing suffice. **BSL-2** covers moderate-risk agents such as *Staphylococcus aureus*, adding restricted access, biohazard signs and biological safety cabinets for aerosol-producing work. **BSL-3** is for agents that can cause serious disease through inhalation, such as *Mycobacterium tuberculosis*, with directional airflow and respiratory protection. **BSL-4** is for dangerous agents with no treatment, such as Ebola virus, with full-body positive-pressure suits.',
'Chemical hazards are communicated by **Safety Data Sheets (SDS)**, which have 16 standardized sections including hazards, first aid, handling and PPE, and by labels with **GHS pictograms**. Biological waste goes in **biohazard** containers, often autoclaved before disposal; sharps go in puncture-resistant sharps containers; chemical waste follows its SDS. Surfaces are commonly disinfected with 10% bleach or 70% ethanol.',
sort('Which biosafety level?',['BSL-1','BSL-2','BSL-3','BSL-4'],[['Classroom E. coli K-12 transformation',0],['Work with Staphylococcus aureus',1],['Culturing Mycobacterium tuberculosis',2],['Research on Ebola virus',3],['Nonpathogenic yeast fermentation',0],['Human blood samples handled with standard precautions',1]])]],
['Using a balance',[
'Balances measure mass. A **top-loading balance** is used for quick weighing to about 0.01 g; an **analytical balance** measures to 0.0001 g and has draft shields. Correct technique: place the balance on a level, stable surface; make sure the pan is clean; place an empty weigh boat or container on the pan and press **tare** (or zero) so the reading returns to 0.00 g; add the substance gradually with a clean spatula; close draft shields on an analytical balance; record the stable reading with units and appropriate significant figures; then clean up any spill. HOSA’s Skill I has competitors weigh empty microcentrifuge tubes, add liquid with micropipets and a transfer pipet, weigh again and calculate the mass of liquid by subtraction, reporting values such as 0.50 g with correct units. The judge checks that the balance was cleared before each weighing.',
'Because water has a density near 1 g/mL, 500 µL of water should weigh about 0.50 g. Weighing pipetted water is therefore a quick way to check a pipet’s accuracy.']],
['Preparing solutions: percent and molarity',[
'A **solute** dissolves in a **solvent** to make a **solution**. **Mass/volume percent (% w/v)** is grams of solute per 100 mL of solution: 100 mL of a 2% NaCl solution contains 2 g NaCl. **Molarity (M)** is moles of solute per liter of solution. To find grams needed: grams = molarity (mol/L) × volume (L) × molecular weight (g/mol). For 500 mL of 0.5 M NaCl (molecular weight 58.44 g/mol): 0.5 × 0.5 × 58.44 = 14.61 g. Dissolve the solute in less than the final volume, then add solvent to the final volume (often in a volumetric flask or graduated cylinder).',
'**Dilutions** use **C₁V₁ = C₂V₂**. To make 100 mL of 1x buffer from a 50x stock: V₁ = 1 × 100 ÷ 50 = 2 mL of stock plus 98 mL of water. The “x” notation describes concentration relative to working strength: 10x is ten times the working concentration. Biotechnology also uses millimolar (mM) and micromolar (µM) units and volume units of microliters (µL): 1 mL = 1,000 µL.',
{t:'w',kind:'mathlab',mode:'dilution',title:'Practice a dilution with C₁V₁ = C₂V₂'}]],
['Worked practice',[
{t:'example',title:'Make a buffer and a salt solution',prompt:'You need 250 mL of 1x TAE buffer from a 50x stock, and 200 mL of a 0.1 M NaCl solution (NaCl = 58.44 g/mol). How do you prepare each?',steps:['TAE: V₁ = C₂V₂ ÷ C₁ = 1x × 250 mL ÷ 50x = 5 mL of 50x stock.','Add 5 mL stock to 245 mL deionized water for 250 mL total.','NaCl: grams = 0.1 mol/L × 0.200 L × 58.44 g/mol = 1.17 g.','Dissolve 1.17 g NaCl in less than 200 mL water, then bring the volume to 200 mL.'],answer:'5 mL of 50x TAE plus 245 mL water; 1.17 g NaCl brought to 200 mL. Label each bottle with name, concentration, date and initials.'},
'Always label solutions with contents, concentration, date and preparer. Record lot numbers of chemicals in the notebook. Errors in solution preparation are among the most common reasons experiments fail, so checking the calculation with a partner before weighing is good practice.',
flip('Solution vocabulary',[['Solute','The substance dissolved'],['Solvent','The liquid that dissolves the solute'],['Molarity','Moles of solute per liter of solution'],['% w/v','Grams of solute per 100 mL of solution'],['Tare','Reset the balance to zero with the container on it'],['10x stock','Ten times the working concentration']])]],
["Spills, exposures and emergencies",["Know the response before you need it. For a **chemical splash to the eyes**, go immediately to the **eyewash station** and flush for at least 15 minutes while holding the eyelids open, then report it. For a large splash on the body, use the **safety shower** and remove contaminated clothing. For a **biological spill**, alert others, cover the spill with paper towels, apply disinfectant such as 10% bleach from the edges inward, wait the required contact time, then clean up and dispose of materials as biohazard waste. **Broken glass** is picked up with a brush and dustpan or tongs, never with hands, and placed in a broken-glass container.","Report every accident or exposure to the instructor or supervisor, even if it seems minor, so it can be documented and treated. Never return chemicals to stock bottles, never pipet by mouth, and keep flammables away from heat sources. These habits protect you, your coworkers and the integrity of experiments."]]
],takeaways:['Enter with closed-toed shoes and don glasses and gloves; clean up in order: waste, disinfect, remove PPE, wash hands.','BSL-1 suits classroom E. coli K-12; SDS sheets and GHS pictograms communicate chemical hazards.','Tare the balance before each weighing; grams = M × L × g/mol; dilute with C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.'],terms:[['Biosafety level','A set of practices and containment for working with biological agents (BSL-1 to BSL-4).'],['Molarity','Moles of solute per liter of solution.'],['Tare','Resetting a balance to zero with a container on the pan.']],mcqs:[q('Which PPE does the HOSA rating sheet require competitors to don after entering?','Safety glasses or goggles and gloves','A full face shield only','A respirator and gown','No PPE is required','Lab coats are optional.'),q('In what order does HOSA rate the clean-up steps?','Dispose of waste, disinfect the area, remove PPE, wash hands','Remove PPE, wash hands, dispose of waste, disinfect','Wash hands, remove PPE, disinfect, dispose','Disinfect, wash hands, dispose, remove PPE','Order is verified by the judge.'),q('Classroom transformation with E. coli K-12 is typically performed at:','BSL-1','BSL-2','BSL-3','BSL-4','K-12 strains are nonpathogenic.'),q('How many grams of NaCl (58.44 g/mol) are needed for 500 mL of 0.5 M solution?','14.61 g','29.22 g','58.44 g','1.461 g','0.5 × 0.5 × 58.44.'),q('How much 50x stock is needed for 100 mL of 1x buffer?','2 mL','50 mL','5 mL','20 mL','1 × 100 ÷ 50.'),q('Before placing a sample on a balance pan with a weigh boat, you should:','Tare the balance with the empty weigh boat','Add the sample first','Turn off the balance','Weigh the boat after the sample','Taring removes the container’s mass.'),q('A 2% w/v NaCl solution contains how much NaCl per 100 mL?','2 g','0.2 g','20 g','2 mg','% w/v is grams per 100 mL.'),q('Which document lists a chemical’s hazards, first aid and handling in 16 sections?','Safety Data Sheet','Laboratory notebook','IND application','Certificate of analysis','SDS sheets standardize hazard information.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would safely prepare 250 mL of 0.2 M NaCl (58.44 g/mol) and then make 50 mL of a 0.05 M dilution, including PPE, balance technique, calculations, labeling and clean-up.',rubric:[['PPE and safety',['shoes','glasses','goggles','gloves']],['Balance technique and calculation',['tare','2.92','grams','58.44']],['Dilution calculation',['c1v1','12.5','37.5','dilut']],['Labeling and clean-up order',['label','dispose','disinfect','remove ppe','wash']]],model:'I would wear closed-toed shoes and put on safety glasses and gloves. The mass needed is 0.2 mol/L × 0.250 L × 58.44 g/mol = 2.92 g. I would place a weigh boat on a level balance, tare it, add 2.92 g of NaCl with a clean spatula, dissolve it in less than 250 mL of water and bring the volume to 250 mL. For the dilution, C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ gives V₁ = 0.05 × 50 ÷ 0.2 = 12.5 mL of stock plus 37.5 mL of water. I would label both containers with contents, concentration, date and initials, then dispose of waste, disinfect the bench, remove my PPE and wash my hands in that order.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_BSL,OSHA_LAB,BIORAD]};

export const B3={title:'Micropipetting and accurate measurement',hook:'A restriction digest uses 10 µL of DNA. A Bradford dilution uses 2 µL of sample. One air bubble or a pipet set to the wrong volume can ruin an experiment—and on HOSA’s rating sheets, judges check four things almost every time liquid moves: the right pipet, the right setting, a proper tip and a transfer with no bubbles or lost sample.',objectives:['Select the correct micropipet for a volume and set it accurately.','Use the first and second stops correctly to aspirate and dispense.','Use transfer pipets and other volumetric tools appropriately.','Check accuracy and precision by weighing pipetted water.'],sections:[
['Choosing the right pipet',[
'Adjustable **micropipets** are sized by range. A **P20** (often 2–20 µL) handles small volumes such as 2, 5 or 10 µL; a **P200** (20–200 µL) handles 50, 100 or 200 µL; a **P1000** (100–1,000 µL) handles 250, 500 or 1,000 µL. Choose the smallest pipet whose range includes the volume, because pipets are most accurate in the upper part of their range. Never turn the volume dial beyond the pipet’s range, which can damage it. HOSA’s Skill I uses a 20–200 µL micropipet for 200 µL and 100 µL transfers and a 100–1,000 µL micropipet for 500 µL, plus a disposable **transfer pipet** (Beral pipet) for an approximate volume.',
'**Transfer pipets** are plastic bulbs with graduations; they are quick but approximate. **Serological pipets** with a pipet aid measure milliliters. **Graduated cylinders** measure larger volumes; read the bottom of the **meniscus** at eye level. **Volumetric flasks** are the most accurate for preparing a solution to an exact volume.',
sort('Choose the best tool',['P20 (2–20 µL)','P200 (20–200 µL)','P1000 (100–1,000 µL)','Graduated cylinder'],[['10 µL of DNA sample',0],['2 µL of protein sample',0],['50 µL of ELISA reagent',1],['98 µL of PBS',1],['250 µL of transformation solution',2],['1 mL of Bradford reagent',2],['200 mL of TAE buffer',3]])]],
['First stop, second stop',[
'A micropipet plunger has two stops. To **aspirate** (draw up) liquid: set the volume, attach a tip firmly, hold the pipet vertically, press the plunger to the **first stop**, place the tip just below the liquid surface, and release the plunger slowly and smoothly. Pause a moment to let liquid fill the tip. To **dispense**, touch the tip to the inside wall of the receiving tube (or into the liquid already there), press slowly to the first stop, then to the **second stop** to blow out the remaining drop, and remove the tip while still holding the plunger down. Only then release the plunger. Releasing it while the tip is still in the liquid draws liquid back up.',
'Common errors include pressing to the second stop before aspirating (which draws up too much), releasing the plunger quickly (which creates bubbles and splashes liquid into the pipet shaft), holding the pipet at a steep angle, and submerging the tip too deeply so liquid clings to the outside. Change tips between different samples or reagents to avoid **cross-contamination**; HOSA judges look for a clean, appropriate tip for each sample.',
order('Pipet a sample correctly',['Choose the micropipet whose range includes the volume','Set the volume on the dial','Attach a clean, appropriate tip','Press to the first stop before entering the liquid','Release slowly to aspirate without bubbles','Dispense to the first stop, then the second stop','Remove the tip while holding the plunger down','Eject the tip into waste'])]],
['Accuracy and precision',[
'**Accuracy** describes how close a measurement is to the true value; **precision** describes how close repeated measurements are to each other. A pipet that delivers 98, 98 and 98 µL when set to 100 µL is precise but slightly inaccurate; one delivering 92, 105 and 101 µL is neither precise nor accurate. Gravimetric checking—weighing water dispensed by a pipet—tests both, because 1 µL of water weighs about 1 mg at room temperature. Facilities **calibrate** pipets on a schedule and record the results.',
'Report measurements with appropriate **significant figures** and units. In HOSA Skill I, the mass of liquid in each tube should be about 0.50 g for the micropipetted tubes, and the judge checks that the competitor used grams and an appropriate number of significant figures. Subtraction of the empty tube mass from the filled tube mass gives the liquid mass.',
{t:'example',title:'Check a pipet by weighing',prompt:'An empty tube weighs 1.02 g. After adding 200 µL, then another 200 µL, then 100 µL of water, it weighs 1.51 g. Is the pipetting accurate?',steps:['Total volume added = 200 + 200 + 100 = 500 µL.','Expected mass of water ≈ 500 mg = 0.50 g.','Measured mass = 1.51 g − 1.02 g = 0.49 g.','The difference of 0.01 g (2%) is small; the pipetting is reasonably accurate.'],answer:'0.49 g measured versus 0.50 g expected: accurate within about 2%. Repeating several times would test precision.'}]],
['Working cleanly with small volumes',[
'Small volumes cling to tube walls. After adding reagents, **mix** by gently pipetting up and down or flicking the tube, then **collect** liquid at the bottom by a brief **pulse spin** in a microcentrifuge or by tapping the tube on the bench. When using a centrifuge, **balance** it by placing tubes of equal volume opposite each other—HOSA judges verify balancing. Label tubes on the top and side before adding anything. Keep enzymes and many reagents on ice. Close tubes tightly before mixing or spinning.',
match('Match the technique to its purpose',[['Pulse spin in a microcentrifuge','Collect liquid at the bottom of the tube'],['Balance tubes opposite each other','Prevent centrifuge damage and imbalance'],['Change tips between samples','Avoid cross-contamination'],['Release the plunger slowly','Avoid bubbles and drawing liquid into the shaft'],['Label tubes before adding liquid','Avoid sample mix-ups'],['Read the bottom of the meniscus','Measure volume accurately in a cylinder']])]],
['Practice the HOSA Skill I sequence',[
'Skill I combines everything in this lesson: enter in closed-toed shoes and don glasses and gloves; label three tubes (for example p200, p1000 and TP); weigh each empty tube after clearing the balance; pipet 200 µL twice and then 100 µL with the 20–200 µL pipet into one tube; pipet 500 µL with the 100–1,000 µL pipet into another; transfer an approximate volume with the transfer pipet into the third; weigh all three tubes; calculate liquid masses in grams with correct significant figures; and finish with the clean-up sequence. Practicing this exact sequence until it is smooth helps you stay within the 15-minute limit.',
kase('A pipetting problem during a skill',{a:{text:'You draw up 200 µL and notice a large air bubble in the tip. What should you do?',choices:[['Dispense the liquid back into its tube and aspirate again slowly','b','Correct. A bubble means the volume is wrong; redo the aspiration.',true],['Dispense it anyway and continue','b','The delivered volume would be inaccurate and the judge checks for bubbles.',false]]},b:{text:'You need to add the same reagent to a second tube that already contains a different sample. What about the tip?',choices:[['Use a fresh tip','c','Correct. A fresh tip prevents carrying the first sample into the reagent stock or second tube.',true],['Reuse the tip to save time','c','Reusing tips risks cross-contamination.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish with clean, bubble-free transfers, weigh the tubes and report 0.50 g with units. Then you clean up in the rated order.',end:true}})]],
["Caring for pipets and other measuring tools",["Micropipets are precision instruments. Store them upright in a rack, never lay them down with liquid in the tip (liquid can run into the shaft), never let the plunger snap back, and never set the volume beyond the stated range. Wipe the exterior with 70% ethanol if contaminated and send pipets for scheduled calibration. Use tips designed for the pipet brand and size; a poor seal leaks and gives wrong volumes. **Filter tips** contain a barrier that protects the pipet shaft from aerosols, which matters in PCR work.","For larger volumes, choose the tool that matches the required accuracy. A **beaker** is only approximate; a **graduated cylinder** is better; a **volumetric flask** or **volumetric pipet** gives the highest accuracy for one fixed volume. Read glassware at eye level at the bottom of the meniscus, and allow liquid to drain fully from pipets as directed. Recording the tool used and its accuracy helps explain small differences between results."]]
],takeaways:['Choose the smallest micropipet whose range includes the volume: P20, P200 or P1000.','Aspirate from the first stop and release slowly; dispense to the first then second stop and remove the tip before releasing.','Check accuracy by weighing water (1 µL ≈ 1 mg); change tips, balance the centrifuge and label tubes first.'],terms:[['First stop','Plunger position used to aspirate the set volume.'],['Second stop','Plunger position that blows out the remaining liquid.'],['Precision','How closely repeated measurements agree.']],mcqs:[q('Which micropipet is best for transferring 10 µL?','P20 (2–20 µL)','P200 (20–200 µL)','P1000 (100–1,000 µL)','A 10 mL serological pipet','Use the smallest pipet that covers the volume.'),q('To aspirate liquid, press the plunger to the:','First stop','Second stop','Halfway point','Tip ejector','The second stop is used only to blow out.'),q('When should you release the plunger after dispensing?','After removing the tip from the liquid','While the tip is still in the liquid','Before reaching the first stop','It does not matter','Releasing in the liquid draws it back up.'),q('About how much does 500 µL of water weigh?','0.50 g','5.0 g','0.05 g','50 g','1 µL of water ≈ 1 mg.'),q('A pipet delivers 98, 98 and 98 µL when set to 100 µL. It is:','Precise but slightly inaccurate','Accurate but imprecise','Neither precise nor accurate','Perfectly accurate','Repeated results agree but miss the target.'),q('Why must tubes be balanced in a microcentrifuge?','To prevent imbalance and damage','To make the run faster','To keep samples cold','To mix samples together','Judges verify balancing.'),q('What is the main reason to change tips between samples?','To avoid cross-contamination','To increase volume','To cool the pipet','To save reagents','Fresh tips keep samples separate.'),q('A brief pulse spin is used to:','Collect liquid at the bottom of the tube','Separate DNA fragments','Sterilize the tube','Heat the sample','It brings droplets down.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to perform HOSA Biotechnology Skill I, including choosing pipets, aspirating and dispensing, weighing, calculating liquid mass and cleaning up, and explain how the results show pipetting accuracy.',rubric:[['PPE and labeling',['glasses','gloves','label','shoes']],['Pipet selection and technique',['20-200','100-1,000','first stop','second stop','bubble','tip']],['Weighing and calculation',['tare','clear','subtract','0.50 g','grams']],['Clean-up order and accuracy interpretation',['dispose','disinfect','remove ppe','wash','accura']]],model:'I would enter in closed-toed shoes, put on glasses and gloves, and label three tubes. After clearing the balance, I would weigh each empty tube and record its mass. Using the 20–200 µL micropipet with a proper tip, I would aspirate from the first stop, release slowly to avoid bubbles and dispense to the first then second stop, adding 200 µL twice and 100 µL to one tube; I would use the 100–1,000 µL pipet for 500 µL in the second tube and a transfer pipet for the third. I would weigh the tubes again and subtract the empty masses; each micropipetted tube should contain about 0.50 g because 1 µL of water weighs about 1 mg, so values close to 0.50 g show accurate pipetting. Finally I would dispose of tips and tubes, disinfect the bench, remove PPE and wash my hands in that order.'},sources:[RULES,BIORAD,OSHA_LAB]};

export const B4={title:'Microbiology, aseptic technique and cell culture',hook:'A plate of bacteria can grow from a single cell to a visible colony overnight. That rapid growth makes microbes powerful tools for biotechnology—and makes contamination a constant threat. Aseptic technique is how scientists grow only the cells they intend to grow.',objectives:['Compare prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells used in biotechnology.','Describe bacterial growth, media and the phases of a growth curve.','Apply aseptic technique and sterilization methods.','Describe mammalian cell culture and contamination control.'],sections:[
['Cells used in biotechnology',[
'**Prokaryotes**, such as bacteria, lack a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles; their DNA is in a single circular chromosome, often with small circular **plasmids**. They divide by **binary fission**, sometimes every 20 minutes for *E. coli* under ideal conditions. **Eukaryotes**, including yeast, plants and animals, have a nucleus and organelles. Biotechnology uses *E. coli* to copy plasmids and make simple proteins; **yeast** (*Saccharomyces cerevisiae*) for fermentation and some proteins; and **mammalian cells**, such as Chinese hamster ovary (CHO) cells, to make complex proteins like monoclonal antibodies that need modifications bacteria cannot perform.',
'Bacteria are described by shape—**cocci** (spheres), **bacilli** (rods) and **spirilla** (spirals)—and by the **Gram stain**, which separates **Gram-positive** bacteria (thick peptidoglycan wall, stain purple) from **Gram-negative** bacteria (thin wall plus outer membrane, stain pink). *E. coli* is a Gram-negative rod.',
match('Match the term',[['Small circular DNA separate from the chromosome','Plasmid'],['Asexual division of a bacterium into two cells','Binary fission'],['Rod-shaped bacteria','Bacilli'],['Thick wall, stains purple','Gram-positive'],['Mammalian cell line used to make antibodies','CHO cells'],['Visible mass of cells from one original cell','Colony']])]],
['Growing bacteria',[
'Bacteria grow in **media** that provide nutrients. **Luria-Bertani (LB) broth** is a liquid medium; **LB agar** is solidified with agar for plates. **Selective media** contain substances, such as the antibiotic ampicillin, that allow only certain cells (those carrying a resistance gene) to grow. Plates are incubated upside down so condensation does not drip onto colonies, often at 37 °C for *E. coli*.',
'A bacterial **growth curve** has four phases: the **lag phase**, when cells adjust and prepare to divide; the **log (exponential) phase**, when cells divide at a constant rate and the population doubles repeatedly; the **stationary phase**, when growth equals death as nutrients run out and wastes build up; and the **death (decline) phase**. Researchers often harvest cells or plasmids during late log phase. Cell density in liquid culture is estimated by **optical density** at 600 nm (OD600) using a spectrophotometer.',
order('Phases of a bacterial growth curve',['Lag phase: cells adapt to the new medium','Log phase: exponential doubling','Stationary phase: growth equals death','Death phase: population declines'])]],
['Aseptic technique and streaking',[
'**Aseptic technique** prevents contamination of cultures and protects the worker. It includes disinfecting the bench, working near a flame or in a biological safety cabinet when appropriate, using sterile loops, pipets and tips, keeping lids on plates and tubes except when needed, holding lids over plates to shield them, and never touching sterile surfaces. Single-use sterile plastic loops are common in classrooms; metal loops are sterilized by flaming until red-hot and cooled before use.',
'A **streak plate** isolates single colonies. Touch a sterile loop to the culture and streak it in the first quadrant; with a fresh or sterilized loop, drag from quadrant one into quadrant two; repeat into quadrants three and four. Each streak dilutes the cells so that the last quadrant has separated **isolated colonies**, each arising from a single cell. Label plates on the bottom (agar side) with name, date, organism and medium.',
order('Streak for isolated colonies',['Label the bottom of the agar plate','Pick up cells with a sterile loop','Streak quadrant 1','With a fresh loop, drag through quadrant 1 into quadrant 2','Repeat into quadrants 3 and 4','Incubate the plate upside down'])]],
['Sterilization and disinfection',[
'**Sterilization** kills all microorganisms, including spores. The **autoclave** uses pressurized steam, commonly at **121 °C and about 15 psi for at least 15–20 minutes**, to sterilize media, glassware and biohazard waste; indicator tape changes color to show exposure. Other methods include dry heat, filtration through 0.2 µm filters for heat-sensitive solutions, and radiation for disposable plastics. **Disinfection** reduces microbes on surfaces using chemicals such as 10% bleach or 70% ethanol but may not kill spores. **Antiseptics** are used on skin.',
sort('Sterilize or disinfect?',['Sterilization','Disinfection'],[['Autoclaving LB agar before pouring plates',0],['Filtering a heat-sensitive antibiotic solution through a 0.2 µm filter',0],['Autoclaving biohazard waste',0],['Wiping the bench with 10% bleach',1],['Spraying 70% ethanol on a work surface',1]])]],
['Mammalian cell culture',[
'Mammalian cells grow in liquid **culture medium** containing nutrients, salts, and often serum, inside **incubators** at 37 °C with about 5% CO₂ to maintain pH. **Adherent cells** grow attached to flask surfaces; **suspension cells** float. When cells fill the surface (become **confluent**), they are **passaged** (split) into new flasks. Work is done in a **biological safety cabinet** with sterile technique. Contamination by bacteria, fungi or mycoplasma can ruin cultures; signs include cloudy medium, sudden pH color change or unusual growth. Large-scale biomanufacturing grows cells in **bioreactors** that control temperature, oxygen, pH and nutrients, followed by **purification** steps such as chromatography to isolate the product.',
kase('A contaminated plate',{a:{text:'Your streak plate shows the expected colonies plus a fuzzy green growth near the edge. What is the most likely explanation?',choices:[['Fungal (mold) contamination from airborne spores or poor technique','b','Correct. Fuzzy, colored growth suggests mold contamination.',true],['The bacteria changed species overnight','b','Bacteria do not become mold.',false]]},b:{text:'What should you do with the plate?',choices:[['Keep it closed and dispose of it as biohazard waste, then review aseptic technique','c','Correct. Do not open contaminated plates; autoclave or dispose per protocol.',true],['Open it and smell it to identify the organism','c','Never open or sniff plates; this risks exposure.',false]]},c:{text:'You discard the plate as biohazard waste, disinfect the bench and repeat the streak with lids kept closed and sterile loops, producing clean isolated colonies.',end:true}})]],
["Counting cells: serial dilution and optical density",["Biotechnologists often need to know how many live cells are in a culture. **Serial dilution plating** dilutes a culture step by step—for example, transferring 100 µL into 900 µL six times to make dilutions of 10⁻¹ through 10⁻⁶—then spreads a measured volume of several dilutions on plates. After incubation, plates with roughly 30 to 300 colonies are counted. Each colony represents one **colony-forming unit (CFU)**. The original concentration equals colonies ÷ (dilution × volume plated). If 150 colonies grow from 0.1 mL of the 10⁻⁶ dilution, the culture contained 150 ÷ (10⁻⁶ × 0.1) = 1.5 × 10⁹ CFU/mL.","A faster estimate uses a **spectrophotometer** to measure **optical density at 600 nm (OD600)**: cloudier cultures scatter more light. OD600 tracks growth through the lag, log and stationary phases, but it counts dead cells too and must be calibrated against plate counts for a particular organism. Laboratories often grow cultures to a target OD before harvesting cells or inducing protein production.",{"t":"check","q":"150 colonies grow when 0.1 mL of a 10⁻⁵ dilution is plated. What is the original concentration?","options":["1.5 × 10⁸ CFU/mL","1.5 × 10⁷ CFU/mL","1.5 × 10⁶ CFU/mL","1.5 × 10⁹ CFU/mL"],"why":"150 ÷ (10⁻⁵ × 0.1) = 1.5 × 10⁸ CFU/mL."}]]
],takeaways:['E. coli, yeast and CHO cells are biotechnology workhorses; plasmids are small circular DNA in bacteria.','Growth curve: lag, log, stationary, death; selective media allow only resistant cells to grow.','Autoclave at about 121 °C and 15 psi; streak for isolated colonies with aseptic technique; incubate plates upside down.'],terms:[['Aseptic technique','Practices that prevent contamination of cultures and protect the worker.'],['Selective medium','Medium that allows only certain cells, such as antibiotic-resistant ones, to grow.'],['Autoclave','Device that sterilizes with pressurized steam.']],mcqs:[q('Small circular DNA molecules in bacteria, separate from the chromosome, are:','Plasmids','Ribosomes','Introns','Codons','Plasmids are used as vectors.'),q('During which growth phase do bacteria divide at a constant, exponential rate?','Log phase','Lag phase','Stationary phase','Death phase','The population doubles repeatedly.'),q('Standard autoclave conditions are about:','121 °C and 15 psi','37 °C and normal pressure','100 °C and 50 psi','4 °C and vacuum','Steam under pressure kills spores.'),q('Why are agar plates incubated upside down?','To keep condensation from dripping onto colonies','To make agar melt','To grow fungi','To speed up growth','Condensation can smear colonies.'),q('A medium containing ampicillin that allows only resistant cells to grow is:','Selective','Differential only','Sterile','Minimal','Selection identifies transformed cells.'),q('Gram-negative bacteria stain:','Pink','Purple','Green','Black','Gram-positive cells stain purple.'),q('Mammalian cells used to make monoclonal antibodies are often:','Chinese hamster ovary (CHO) cells','E. coli','Red blood cells','Yeast spores','CHO cells perform complex protein modifications.'),q('The purpose of streaking a plate in quadrants is to:','Obtain isolated colonies from single cells','Kill bacteria','Measure DNA','Make a gel','Each quadrant dilutes the cells.')],frq:{prompt:'You must grow a pure culture of E. coli on LB agar. Explain the aseptic technique and streaking method you would use, how you would recognize and handle contamination, and how sterilization supports the work.',rubric:[['Aseptic technique',['sterile','lid','disinfect','loop','aseptic']],['Streaking for isolated colonies',['quadrant','isolated','streak','colon']],['Recognizing and handling contamination',['contamin','fuzzy','biohazard','dispose']],['Sterilization methods',['autoclave','121','15 psi','bleach','ethanol']]],model:'I would disinfect the bench, wear PPE, and use sterile loops, keeping plate lids closed except when streaking. I would streak quadrant one from the culture, then use a fresh loop to drag cells from quadrant one into two, and repeat into three and four, so the final quadrant has isolated colonies that each came from a single cell. I would label the bottom of the plate and incubate it upside down at 37 °C. Contamination appears as colonies with different shapes or colors, such as fuzzy mold; I would keep that plate closed, dispose of it as biohazard waste and review my technique. The LB agar and waste are sterilized in an autoclave at about 121 °C and 15 psi, and surfaces are disinfected with 10% bleach or 70% ethanol.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_BSL,BIORAD]};

export const B5={title:'DNA structure and restriction digestion',hook:'A restriction enzyme scans a DNA molecule and cuts only where it finds an exact sequence of a few letters, such as GAATTC. That precision made genetic engineering possible and is the basis of DNA fingerprinting experiments in HOSA’s skills round.',objectives:['Describe DNA structure, including base pairing, hydrogen bonds and antiparallel strands.','Explain how restriction enzymes recognize and cut DNA, producing sticky or blunt ends.','Set up a restriction digestion reaction following HOSA Skill II.','Predict fragment numbers from restriction sites.'],sections:[
['The structure of DNA',[
'**Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA)** is made of **nucleotides**, each containing a **deoxyribose sugar**, a **phosphate group** and one of four **nitrogenous bases**: adenine (A), thymine (T), guanine (G) and cytosine (C). Nucleotides link through **phosphodiester bonds** between the sugar of one and the phosphate of the next, forming a **sugar-phosphate backbone**. Two strands wind into a **double helix**, described by James Watson and Francis Crick in 1953 using X-ray data from Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins. The strands are **antiparallel**: one runs 5′ to 3′ and the other 3′ to 5′.',
'Bases pair by **complementary base pairing**: A with T (two **hydrogen bonds**) and G with C (three hydrogen bonds). Hydrogen bonds between base pairs hold the two strands together—answering the HOSA sample question. Because G–C pairs have three hydrogen bonds, DNA rich in G and C needs more heat to separate. **Chargaff’s rules** state that in double-stranded DNA the amount of A equals T and G equals C. **Purines** (A and G) have two rings; **pyrimidines** (C and T) have one ring.',
match('DNA structure',[['Bond holding base pairs together','Hydrogen bond'],['Bond linking nucleotides in the backbone','Phosphodiester bond'],['Base that pairs with adenine','Thymine'],['Base pair with three hydrogen bonds','G–C'],['Strands running in opposite directions','Antiparallel'],['Two-ring bases A and G','Purines']])]],
['How restriction enzymes work',[
'**Restriction enzymes** (restriction endonucleases) are proteins bacteria use to cut invading viral DNA. Each recognizes a specific **recognition site**, typically 4–8 base pairs, that is usually **palindromic**: it reads the same 5′ to 3′ on both strands. *Eco*RI recognizes 5′-GAATTC-3′ and cuts between G and A, leaving single-stranded overhangs called **sticky ends** (AATT). *Hin*dIII recognizes AAGCTT and also leaves sticky ends. Some enzymes, such as *Sma*I (CCCGGG), cut straight across and leave **blunt ends**. Sticky ends from the same enzyme can pair with each other, and **DNA ligase** seals the backbone—this is how genes are inserted into plasmids. Bacteria protect their own DNA by **methylating** their recognition sites.',
'Enzyme names come from the organism: *Eco*RI from *Escherichia coli* strain RY13, the first enzyme identified from that strain (Roman numeral I). Restriction enzymes need specific **buffers** and temperatures—usually 37 °C—and are kept on ice before use because they are temperature-sensitive.',
sort('Sticky or blunt ends?',['Sticky ends','Blunt ends'],[['EcoRI cutting GAATTC between G and A',0],['HindIII cutting AAGCTT',0],['BamHI cutting GGATCC',0],['SmaI cutting CCCGGG in the middle',1],['EcoRV cutting GATATC in the middle',1]])]],
['Predicting fragments',[
'The number of fragments depends on the number of cuts and whether the DNA is **linear** or **circular**. A linear molecule cut at *n* sites produces *n* + 1 fragments. A circular plasmid cut at *n* sites produces *n* fragments: one cut opens the circle into a single linear piece. In DNA fingerprinting, differences between individuals in the number or position of restriction sites produce different fragment patterns, called **restriction fragment length polymorphisms (RFLPs)**. Modern forensic DNA profiling mainly uses **short tandem repeats (STRs)** analyzed by PCR, but RFLP-style classroom experiments teach the same logic of comparing band patterns.',
{t:'example',title:'Count the fragments',prompt:'A 10,000 bp linear DNA molecule has EcoRI sites at positions 2,000 and 7,500. A 5,000 bp circular plasmid has one EcoRI site. How many fragments, and what sizes?',steps:['Linear DNA with 2 cuts → 3 fragments.','Sizes: 2,000 bp; 7,500 − 2,000 = 5,500 bp; 10,000 − 7,500 = 2,500 bp.','Circular plasmid with 1 cut → 1 linear fragment of 5,000 bp.'],answer:'Linear: three fragments of 2,000, 5,500 and 2,500 bp. Plasmid: one 5,000 bp fragment.'},
{t:'check',q:'A circular plasmid is cut at three restriction sites. How many fragments result?',options:['Three','Four','Two','Six'],why:'Circular DNA cut n times gives n fragments.'}]],
['Setting up a restriction digest (HOSA Skill II)',[
'HOSA’s Skill II follows a Bio-Rad crime-scene style protocol. After entering in closed-toed shoes and donning glasses and gloves, the competitor **labels six microcentrifuge tubes** to match the DNA samples, **pipets 10 µL of each DNA sample** into its tube using a 20 µL micropipet set to 10 µL with a clean tip for each sample, then **adds 10 µL of enzyme mix (ENZ)** to each tube with a fresh tip each time, mixing by pipetting up and down two or three times. The competitor **caps each tube and mixes by flicking**, then **pulse-spins** in a balanced microcentrifuge or taps tubes on the table to collect liquid at the bottom. Digests are then incubated (commonly at 37 °C) for the time in the protocol, after which samples are ready for gel electrophoresis. The skill ends with the standard clean-up sequence.',
order('Restriction digest setup (HOSA Skill II)',['Enter in closed-toed shoes and don glasses and gloves','Label six tubes to match the DNA samples','Pipet 10 µL of each DNA sample into its tube with a clean tip','Add 10 µL of enzyme mix to each tube with a fresh tip and mix by pipetting','Cap tubes and mix by flicking','Pulse-spin in a balanced microcentrifuge or tap to collect liquid','Incubate as the protocol directs','Dispose of waste, disinfect, remove PPE and wash hands'])]],
['Why controls and care matter',[
'A digest can fail if the enzyme is inactive (left warm), the buffer is wrong, DNA is contaminated, or volumes are inaccurate. **Controls** help interpret results: an **uncut** DNA sample shows what undigested DNA looks like, and a **DNA ladder** (size standard) on the gel allows fragment sizing. Cross-contamination—carrying one suspect’s DNA into another’s tube—would make a fingerprint comparison meaningless, which is why HOSA judges watch for a clean tip for every sample. Keep enzyme tubes on ice and return them promptly.',
flip('Restriction vocabulary',[['Restriction enzyme','Protein that cuts DNA at a specific recognition sequence'],['Recognition site','The short sequence an enzyme recognizes, often palindromic'],['Sticky ends','Single-stranded overhangs left by staggered cuts'],['Blunt ends','Straight cuts with no overhang'],['DNA ligase','Enzyme that seals DNA backbones'],['RFLP','Different fragment patterns caused by sequence differences']])]],
["Extracting and measuring DNA",["Before DNA can be cut or copied, it must be **extracted**. A basic extraction **lyses** cells with detergent to break membranes, uses salt to help separate DNA from proteins, may add **protease** to digest proteins, and precipitates DNA with cold **ethanol or isopropanol**, where long DNA strands appear as white, stringy material. Plasmid DNA is purified from bacteria by **alkaline lysis** and spin columns that bind DNA to silica.","DNA quantity and purity are measured by **UV spectrophotometry**: nucleic acids absorb most strongly at **260 nm**, and proteins at 280 nm. An **A260/A280 ratio** near **1.8** indicates relatively pure DNA; lower ratios suggest protein contamination. Concentration is estimated because an absorbance of 1.0 at 260 nm corresponds to about 50 µg/mL of double-stranded DNA. Clean, measured DNA makes restriction digests and PCR reliable."]]
],takeaways:['DNA: sugar-phosphate backbone with phosphodiester bonds; hydrogen bonds pair A–T (2) and G–C (3); strands are antiparallel.','Restriction enzymes cut palindromic sites, leaving sticky or blunt ends; ligase joins fragments.','Linear DNA cut n times gives n + 1 fragments; circular gives n. In Skill II, use a clean tip per sample, flick, pulse-spin and incubate.'],terms:[['Hydrogen bond','Weak bond that pairs complementary bases.'],['Palindromic sequence','A sequence that reads the same 5′→3′ on both strands.'],['Sticky ends','Single-stranded overhangs from a staggered cut.']],mcqs:[q('What type of bond connects nitrogenous base pairs and holds the two strands of DNA together?','Hydrogen','Nitrogenous','Oxygen','Carbon','This is the HOSA sample question.'),q('How many hydrogen bonds join a G–C base pair?','Three','Two','One','Four','A–T pairs have two.'),q('EcoRI recognizes which sequence?','GAATTC','AAGCTT','CCCGGG','GGATCC','EcoRI cuts between G and A.'),q('A linear DNA molecule with three restriction sites produces how many fragments?','Four','Three','Two','Six','Linear: n + 1.'),q('Which enzyme seals sticky ends together?','DNA ligase','Taq polymerase','Restriction enzyme','Helicase','Ligase forms phosphodiester bonds.'),q('In HOSA Skill II, how much of each DNA sample is pipetted into its tube?','10 µL','100 µL','2 µL','50 µL','A 20 µL pipet is set to 10 µL.'),q('Why are restriction enzymes kept on ice?','They are temperature-sensitive and lose activity when warm','To make them sticky','To kill bacteria','To change their sequence','Cold preserves activity.'),q('Strands of DNA that run in opposite 5′→3′ directions are:','Antiparallel','Parallel','Palindromic','Complementary only','One runs 5′→3′, the other 3′→5′.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a restriction enzyme cuts DNA, predict the fragments when a 6,000 bp linear molecule is cut at positions 1,500 and 4,000, and describe how you would set up the digestion in HOSA Skill II while avoiding contamination.',rubric:[['Enzyme recognition and cutting',['recognition','palindrom','sticky','blunt','site']],['Fragment prediction',['three','1,500','2,500','2,000']],['Skill II setup steps',['label','10 µl','enzyme','flick','pulse','incubat']],['Contamination control',['clean tip','fresh tip','cross-contamination','ice']]],model:'A restriction enzyme recognizes a specific, usually palindromic sequence and cuts both strands, leaving sticky ends with overhangs or blunt ends. Cutting a 6,000 bp linear molecule at 1,500 and 4,000 gives three fragments: 1,500 bp, 2,500 bp and 2,000 bp. For Skill II, I would wear shoes, glasses and gloves, label six tubes to match the samples, pipet 10 µL of each DNA sample with a clean tip, add 10 µL of enzyme mix with a fresh tip for each tube and mix by pipetting, cap and flick the tubes, pulse-spin in a balanced microcentrifuge, and incubate as directed. Using a new tip for every sample and keeping the enzyme on ice prevents cross-contamination and preserves activity, then I would clean up in the rated order.'},sources:[RULES,NHGRI_DNA,NHGRI_RE,BIORAD]};
